S[36] = dict(S[10])
S[36].update(title='Track fragments require explicit reconnection evidence',figure=fig('dlc','dlc3ab','Fig. 3(a–e)','Ground-truth tracks, reconstructed tracks and local matching.'),cite='Lauer et al. (2022)',refs=[ref('dlc')],body=[
'A **tracklet** is a short sequence of detections linked as belonging to one animal. Occlusion can end a tracklet even though the animal remains in the scene. Lauer and colleagues first established local connections between neighboring frames, then evaluated how separated fragments could be reconnected.',
'The stitching procedure used shape similarity, spatial proximity, movement predictions and similarities in trajectory dynamics. Each supplied a different kind of evidence that two fragments belonged to the same individual. A nearby fragment could still be the wrong match if its shape or movement conflicted with the earlier animal.',
'The study compared stitched tracks with simpler box and ellipse trackers. Combining fragments improved continuity and reduced identity switches. Explicitly separating local tracking from later stitching made long behavioral records recoverable when uninterrupted frame-to-frame matching failed.'])
S[38] = dict(S[16])
S[38].update(title='Error distributions locate difficult anatomical landmarks',figure=fig('sleap','sleap2','Fig. 2(a–g)','Body-part localization errors and manually annotated landmarks.'),body=[
'SLEAP evaluated predicted landmarks against manual locations in held-out frames. A **localization error** is the distance between those positions in the image. Measuring errors for individual body parts exposed differences that a single average accuracy could conceal.',
'Visible appendages, overlapping limbs and changes in body orientation created different recognition demands. The distributions included both small common deviations and less frequent larger mistakes. Reporting an upper portion of the error distribution described difficult frames as well as typical performance.',
'The study also expressed errors relative to body size, linking pixels to the animal being recorded. A distance in image coordinates acquires behavioral significance through anatomical scale. Part-specific evaluation connected the tracker’s output to the precision needed for posture or contact measurements.'])
S[40] = dict(S[22])
S[40].update(title='Participation timing separates activity from accuracy',figure=fig('auto','auto2','Fig. 2(a–f)','Self-initiated trials and discrimination across the daily cycle.'),cite='Erskine et al. (2019)',refs=[ref('auto')],body=[
'AutonoMouse logged when each individually identified mouse entered the task and completed trials. Animals performed more trials during their dark-phase activity period. Continuous monitoring captured these self-selected schedules instead of restricting observation to a fixed daytime session.',
'Trial frequency and discrimination accuracy were measured separately across the daily cycle. More participation at a particular hour did not translate into a matching systematic rise in accuracy. The amount of recorded behavior and the quality of stimulus discrimination therefore described different features of the animal’s activity.',
'Animals often performed runs of consecutive trials, then returned to the shared cage. Linking those bouts to individual identity preserved differences in participation schedules. Automated longitudinal records connected access, activity and task performance without equating frequent participation with better discrimination.'])
S[18]['title']='Measured delay differs from model inference speed'
S[18]['body'][0]='Pereira and colleagues timed a live tracking pipeline that measured distance between two flies and passed its output through a data-acquisition loop. The round-trip delay was approximately 71 milliseconds. Aligning the online measurement with the offline trace quantified how old the reported body configuration was when it became available to the controller.'
S[18]['body'][2]='Comparing the online distance trace with the offline trace exposed the lag of the full pipeline. A trigger based on an earlier pose acts on animals that have continued moving. Delay calibration connected the timestamp of a tracked social configuration to the experimental event delivered in response, rather than treating model speed as the complete timing measurement.'
S[19]['title']='Social geometry specifies an intervention trigger'
S[32]['title']='Optical signals align detected bouts with video'
S[32]['body']=[
'A **position-sensitive detector** measured an optical signal associated with tail movement in restrained zebrafish. Trivedi and Bollmann compared this signal with tail angles extracted from high-speed video. A threshold crossing supplied a rapid bout-detection event while the video retained the movement’s detailed shape.',
'The two readouts served different measurement purposes. The detector identified an event quickly enough to trigger a display update; body tracking described which tail segments moved and when. Aligning their time courses tied the fast sensor event to the motor behavior visible in the recording.',
'The resulting alignment placed stimulus position, detector voltage and tail-angle changes on a common time axis. That synchronization made the timing of a sensory intervention experimentally interpretable. A sensor threshold alone would identify an event without preserving the full kinematic evidence for the action that caused it.']
S[33]['title']='Eye convergence accompanies the simulated approach'
S[33]['body'][0]='Trivedi and Bollmann compared eye angles during capture of real paramecia with angles during virtual-prey sequences. The restrained preparation permitted high-resolution tracking while the displayed target changed after detected swims. Eye convergence supplied an additional motor readout beyond the presence of a tail bout.'
S[33]['body'][2]='A comparison condition held target size and speed constant. Its altered inter-bout timing connected the sequence to updated visual information rather than to a fixed count of preceding swims. Combining eye angles and bout timing distinguished a coordinated capture-like sequence from an isolated orienting movement.'
S[34]['title']='Residual target error predicts motor readiness'
S[35]['title']='Movement phase changes visual reaction time'
S[37]['title']='Body placement provides a depth-dependent readout'
S[37]['body'][0]='Feord and colleagues delivered eye-specific moving shrimp images to cuttlefish through colored filters. **Binocular disparity**, the difference between the two eyes’ images, ranged from negative to positive values. Increasing positive disparity placed the apparent prey in front of the screen under the tested geometry. Cuttlefish positioned themselves farther from the screen before launching their tentacles.'
S[39]['title']='Time budgets localize a perceptual impairment'
S[42]['title']='Contrast reversal tests interocular image comparison'
